[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Database Normalization in Oracle DB (1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Normalization is a database design technique that reduces data redundancy and eliminates undesirable characteristics like Insertion, Update and Deletion Anomalies. It divides larger tables into smaller tables and links them using relationships. The purpose of Normalization in Oracle, as in other databases, is to isolate data so that amendments to an attribute (column) can be made in only one table."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Normalization Forms"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Normalization is typically achieved through a series of normalization forms. We will cover the first four: 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, and BCNF."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"1NF (First Normal Form)"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"A table is in 1NF if it satisfies the following conditions:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Each column contains only atomic values (indivisible values)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"There are no repeating groups of columns."},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Example: Consider a table 'Employees' with a 'Skills' column that can contain multiple skills separated by commas."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE Employees (EmployeeID NUMBER, Name VARCHAR2(50), Skills VARCHAR2(100));

INSERT INTO Employees VALUES (1, 'John Doe', 'Java,Oracle,SQL');"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To normalize this to 1NF, we would create a separate table for skills:"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE Employees (EmployeeID NUMBER, Name VARCHAR2(50));
CREATE TABLE EmployeeSkills (EmployeeID NUMBER, Skill VARCHAR2(50));

INSERT INTO Employees VALUES (1, 'John Doe');
INSERT INTO EmployeeSkills VALUES (1, 'Java');
INSERT INTO EmployeeSkills VALUES (1, 'Oracle');
INSERT INTO EmployeeSkills VALUES (1, 'SQL');"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"2NF (Second Normal Form)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"A table is in 2NF if it satisfies the following conditions:"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"It is in 1NF."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"All non-key attributes are fully functionally dependent on the primary key.  This means that no non-key attribute can be determined by only part of the primary key (the primary key must be a composite key)."},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Example: Consider a table 'OrderDetails' with columns OrderID, ProductID (composite primary key), ProductName, and Quantity.  ProductName is dependent only on ProductID, which is part of the primary key."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE OrderDetails (OrderID NUMBER, ProductID NUMBER, ProductName VARCHAR2(50), Quantity NUMBER, PRIMARY KEY (OrderID, ProductID));

INSERT INTO OrderDetails VALUES (100, 1, 'Laptop', 1);
INSERT INTO OrderDetails VALUES (100, 2, 'Mouse', 2);"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"To normalize this to 2NF, we would create a separate table for products:"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE OrderDetails (OrderID NUMBER, ProductID NUMBER, Quantity NUMBER, PRIMARY KEY (OrderID, ProductID));
CREATE TABLE Products (ProductID NUMBER PRIMARY KEY, ProductName VARCHAR2(50));

INSERT INTO OrderDetails VALUES (100, 1, 1);
INSERT INTO OrderDetails VALUES (100, 2, 2);
INSERT INTO Products VALUES (1, 'Laptop');
INSERT INTO Products VALUES (2, 'Mouse');"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"3NF (Third Normal Form)"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"A table is in 3NF if it satisfies the following conditions:"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"It is in 2NF."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"No non-key attribute is transitively dependent on the primary key. This means that no non-key attribute can be determined by another non-key attribute."},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Example: Consider a table 'Customers' with columns CustomerID (primary key), CustomerName, City, and ZipCode. ZipCode is dependent on City."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE Customers (CustomerID NUMBER PRIMARY KEY, CustomerName VARCHAR2(50), City VARCHAR2(50), ZipCode VARCHAR2(10));

INSERT INTO Customers VALUES (1, 'Alice Smith', 'New York', '10001');
INSERT INTO Customers VALUES (2, 'Bob Johnson', 'Los Angeles', '90001');"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"To normalize this to 3NF, we would create a separate table for cities and zip codes:"},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE Customers (CustomerID NUMBER PRIMARY KEY, CustomerName VARCHAR2(50), CityID NUMBER);
CREATE TABLE Cities (CityID NUMBER PRIMARY KEY, City VARCHAR2(50), ZipCode VARCHAR2(10));

INSERT INTO Customers VALUES (1, 'Alice Smith', 1);
INSERT INTO Customers VALUES (2, 'Bob Johnson', 2);
INSERT INTO Cities VALUES (1, 'New York', '10001');
INSERT INTO Cities VALUES (2, 'Los Angeles', '90001');"},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"BCNF (Boyce-Codd Normal Form)"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"A table is in BCNF if it satisfies the following conditions:"},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"For every functional dependency X -> Y, X must be a superkey."},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"BCNF is a stronger version of 3NF. A table that is in 3NF is also in BCNF unless it has multiple overlapping candidate keys."},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Example: Consider a table 'CourseAssignments' with columns StudentID, Course, Instructor.  Assume that each course has only one instructor, and each student is assigned to an instructor for a course.  Also, assume that an instructor can teach multiple courses.  The functional dependencies are (StudentID, Course) -> Instructor and Instructor -> Course.  StudentID is not a superkey, and Instructor is not a superkey. Therefore, this table is not in BCNF."},{"order":34,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE CourseAssignments (StudentID NUMBER, Course VARCHAR2(50), Instructor VARCHAR2(50), PRIMARY KEY (StudentID, Course));

INSERT INTO CourseAssignments VALUES (1, 'Math', 'Dr. Smith');
INSERT INTO CourseAssignments VALUES (2, 'Physics', 'Dr. Brown');"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"To normalize this to BCNF, we would create two tables:"},{"order":36,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE StudentAssignments (StudentID NUMBER, Instructor VARCHAR2(50), PRIMARY KEY (StudentID));
CREATE TABLE InstructorCourses (Instructor VARCHAR2(50), Course VARCHAR2(50), PRIMARY KEY (Instructor));

INSERT INTO StudentAssignments VALUES (1, 'Dr. Smith');
INSERT INTO StudentAssignments VALUES (2, 'Dr. Brown');
INSERT INTO InstructorCourses VALUES ('Dr. Smith', 'Math');
INSERT INTO InstructorCourses VALUES ('Dr. Brown', 'Physics');"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"By applying these normalization forms, you can design a more efficient and reliable Oracle database."}]
